-- Node: ca-special-meds (1 field(s) changed)
UPDATE decision_nodes SET data = '{"type":"info","module":5,"title":"Situation-Specific Medications","body":"**Beyond epinephrine — give when indicated:**\n\n• [Sodium bicarbonate](#/drug/sodium-bicarbonate/hyperkalemia) 1-2 mEq/kg IV:\n  Wide QRS / TCA toxicity, known hyperkalemia, prolonged arrest, pre-existing metabolic acidosis\n\n• **Calcium** (1-2g calcium gluconate or 1g calcium chloride IV):\n  Hyperkalemia, calcium channel blocker OD, hypermagnesemia\n\n• [Magnesium sulfate](#/drug/magnesium-sulfate/torsades) 2g IV over 1-2 min (torsades: repeat 2g at 5-15 min if it persists, then start a continuous infusion — a bolus alone wears off and TdP recurs):\n  Refractory VF, torsades de pointes (polymorphic VT + long QT)\n\n• **Lipid emulsion** (Intralipid 20%) 1.5 mL/kg bolus:\n  Local anesthetic systemic toxicity (LAST)\n\n**Do NOT routinely give NaHCO3 or calcium** — only for specific indications above [5][8].","citation":[5,8],"next":"ca-rosc","summary":"NaHCO3 wide QRS, Ca CCB, Mg TdP, Intralipid LAST"}'::jsonb
WHERE id = 'ca-special-meds' AND tree_id = 'cardiac-arrest';

-- Node: chs-start (1 field(s) changed)
UPDATE decision_nodes SET data = '{"type":"info","module":1,"title":"Cannabinoid Hyperemesis Syndrome","body":"[CHS Steps Summary](#/info/chs-summary)\n\nCHS is a clinical syndrome in chronic cannabis users characterized by cyclic nausea/vomiting and compulsive hot water bathing (~90% of patients). Traditional antiemetics (ondansetron) usually **FAIL**. GRACE-4 guidelines recommend butyrophenones as first-line therapy. Definitive treatment is cannabis cessation.\n\n*Basis:* Syndrome description derives from the original Allen 2004 case series (n=9) and the Simonetto 2012 Mayo series (n=98) [1,9]; treatment framing derives from SAEM GRACE-4 (2024) recommendations 3a and 3b, both graded **very low certainty of evidence** [2]. Note the hot-water-bathing figure: in Simonetto the behaviour was documented in 57/98 patients, and 52 of those 57 (91%) reported relief — the ~90% figure is the relief rate among those documented, not the prevalence in all CHS patients (52/98 = 53%) [1].","citation":[1,2,9],"next":"chs-presentation","summary":"CHS: cyclic vomiting in chronic cannabis users — ondansetron usually FAILS, use butyrophenones"}'::jsonb
WHERE id = 'chs-start' AND tree_id = 'chs';

-- Node: chs-presentation (2 field(s) changed)
UPDATE decision_nodes SET data = '{"type":"question","module":1,"title":"Clinical Phase","body":"Which phase is the patient presenting in?\n\n**Prodromal:** Morning nausea, food aversion, vague abdominal discomfort\n**Hyperemetic:** Intense nausea/vomiting, compulsive hot bathing, significant dehydration\n**Recovery:** Resolving symptoms with cannabis abstinence\n\n*Basis:* The three-phase construct (prodromal / hyperemetic / recovery) is a descriptive framework from narrative and systematic reviews of case series [1,6,7]; it is **not a validated staging instrument** and has no prospectively tested inter-rater reliability or outcome linkage. Phase assignment is clinical judgement.","citation":[1,6,7],"options":[{"label":"Prodromal","description":"Morning nausea, food aversion, mild symptoms","next":"chs-diagnostic-criteria"},{"label":"Hyperemetic","description":"Intense vomiting, hot water bathing, dehydration","urgency":"urgent","next":"chs-hyperemetic"},{"label":"Recovery / Follow-up","description":"Symptom resolution with abstinence","next":"chs-diagnostic-criteria"}],"summary":"Identify phase: prodromal (morning nausea), hyperemetic (profuse vomiting), or recovery (abstinent)"}'::jsonb
WHERE id = 'chs-presentation' AND tree_id = 'chs';

-- Node: chs-hyperemetic (1 field(s) changed)
UPDATE decision_nodes SET data = '{"type":"info","module":1,"title":"Hyperemetic Phase","body":"Profuse vomiting (often >7 episodes/day), diffuse abdominal pain, compulsive hot water bathing for relief, significant volume depletion.\n\n**Complications to screen for:**\n• AKI from dehydration\n• Electrolyte abnormalities (hypokalemia, hyponatremia, hypomagnesemia)\n• Mallory-Weiss tear (hematemesis)\n• Boerhaave syndrome (rare but life-threatening — chest pain, subcutaneous emphysema after forceful vomiting)\n\nWeight loss common with recurrent episodes.\n\n*Basis:* Complication list is compiled from CHS case series and narrative reviews [1,6,7] plus a forensic series of fatal CHS cases in which dehydration, electrolyte derangement and cardiac arrest were the mechanisms of death [11]. There is **no prospective cohort quantifying the incidence** of AKI, Mallory-Weiss tear or Boerhaave syndrome in CHS; these are recognised sequelae of severe protracted vomiting from any cause, applied here by mechanism rather than by CHS-specific data. The \">7 episodes/day\" figure is descriptive of published series, not a diagnostic threshold [1,6].","citation":[1,6,7,11],"next":"chs-diagnostic-criteria","summary":"Screen for complications: AKI, electrolyte abnormalities, Mallory-Weiss tear, Boerhaave"}'::jsonb
WHERE id = 'chs-hyperemetic' AND tree_id = 'chs';

-- Node: chs-diagnostic-criteria (2 field(s) changed)
UPDATE decision_nodes SET data = '{"type":"info","module":1,"title":"Diagnostic Criteria","body":"**CHS is a clinical diagnosis of exclusion.** No standardized ED diagnostic criteria exist.\n\n**Simonetto Criteria (2012):**\n1. Long-term cannabis use (usually daily, >1 year)\n2. Cyclic nausea and vomiting\n3. Symptom resolution with cannabis cessation\n4. No alternative diagnosis\n\n**Supportive features:**\n• Compulsive hot water bathing (~90%)\n• Age <50 at onset\n• Morning predominance of nausea\n• Weight loss >5 kg\n• Cannabis use ≥weekly for ≥1 year\n\n**Key distinction from Cyclic Vomiting Syndrome (CVS):** [Differential Diagnosis](#/info/chs-ddx)\n\n*Basis:* The criteria above are the major/supportive criteria **proposed** by Simonetto et al. from a retrospective single-centre series of 98 patients [1]. They have never been prospectively validated and carry no published sensitivity or specificity. Two figures warrant care when quoting them at the bedside: Simonetto documented cannabis use **more than once weekly** in 71/75 (95%) and use for **more than 2 years** in 25/37 (68%) — \"daily, >1 year\" is a paraphrase, not the reported denominator; and the hot-water-bathing figure is the relief rate (52/57, 91%) among patients in whom the behaviour was recorded, not the prevalence across the cohort [1]. The CHS-versus-CVS distinction follows the ANMS/CVSA supplement [14] and the systematic review by Sorensen et al. [6].","citation":[1,6,14],"next":"chs-red-flags","summary":"Clinical diagnosis of exclusion — daily cannabis >1 year, cyclic vomiting, hot water bathing relief","skippable":true}'::jsonb
WHERE id = 'chs-diagnostic-criteria' AND tree_id = 'chs';

-- Node: chs-red-flags (2 field(s) changed)
UPDATE decision_nodes SET data = '{"type":"question","module":2,"title":"Red Flags Present?","body":"Screen for features suggesting an alternative diagnosis:\n• Hematemesis or melena\n• Severe localized abdominal pain (RUQ, RLQ, epigastric)\n• Peritoneal signs (guarding, rigidity, rebound)\n• Bilious emesis\n• Fever >38.5°C\n• Jaundice\n• Age of first episode >50\n• No cannabis use history\n\n[Differential Diagnosis](#/info/chs-ddx)\n\n*Basis:* This is a **consensus red-flag list**, not a validated decision rule — no published instrument stratifies risk of an alternative diagnosis in suspected CHS, and none of these features has a reported likelihood ratio in this population. The individual features are drawn from the CHS diagnosis-of-exclusion literature [6,7] and the 2025 Emergency Medicine Practice review of cannabis-related emergencies [3]. Absence of red flags does not exclude organic disease; CHS remains a diagnosis of exclusion [6].","citation":[3,6,7],"options":[{"label":"Red flags present","description":"Features suggest alternative diagnosis","urgency":"urgent","next":"chs-organic-workup"},{"label":"No red flags","description":"Clinical picture consistent with CHS","next":"chs-labs"}],"summary":"Screen for red flags: hematemesis, localized pain, peritoneal signs, fever, jaundice, age >50"}'::jsonb
WHERE id = 'chs-red-flags' AND tree_id = 'chs';

-- Node: chs-organic-workup (2 field(s) changed)
UPDATE decision_nodes SET data = '{"type":"info","module":2,"title":"Alternative Diagnosis Workup","body":"Red flags present — pursue targeted workup:\n\n**Differential Diagnosis:**\n• **Cyclic Vomiting Syndrome** — no cannabis association, may respond to triptans\n• **Gastroparesis** — worse postprandially, delayed gastric emptying study\n• **Cannabis withdrawal** — occurs AFTER cessation, not during use\n• **Biliary disease** — RUQ pain, Murphy sign, check LFTs/RUQ US\n• **Pancreatitis** — epigastric radiating to back, check lipase\n• **Bowel obstruction** — distension, absent bowel sounds, imaging\n• **Pregnancy** — check hCG\n• **Adrenal insufficiency** — morning nausea overlap, check cortisol\n\nOrder imaging and labs as clinically indicated.\n\n*Basis:* The differential list is compiled from the CHS systematic review by Sorensen et al. [6], the ANMS/CVSA cyclic-vomiting supplement that separates CVS from CHS [14], and the 2025 Emergency Medicine Practice cannabis review [3]; long-term management and gastroparesis overlap follow Gajendran et al. [20]. **No CHS-specific diagnostic yield data exist** for any of these tests — \"order imaging and labs as clinically indicated\" is a good-practice statement based on the pretest probability of the alternative diagnosis you are entertaining, not on a CHS-derived rule.","citation":[3,6,14,20],"next":"chs-organic-found","summary":"Red flags: pursue targeted workup — biliary, pancreatitis, SBO, pregnancy, adrenal insufficiency","skippable":true}'::jsonb
WHERE id = 'chs-organic-workup' AND tree_id = 'chs';

-- Node: chs-organic-found (2 field(s) changed)
UPDATE decision_nodes SET data = '{"type":"result","module":2,"title":"Alternative Diagnosis Found","body":"Workup reveals an alternative etiology for the patient''s symptoms.\n\n*Basis:* \"Diagnosis of exclusion\" is the defining framing of CHS in the systematic review by Sorensen et al. [6] and in Richards'' ED-focused review [7]; the same framing is carried in the 2025 Emergency Medicine Practice review [3]. There is **no published rule** for how much workup is enough before labelling a patient CHS, and no study of the misdiagnosis rate when an alternative cause is found. Treat the identified cause on its own merits, not on CHS pathways.","citation":[3,6,7],"recommendation":"Treat the underlying cause. CHS remains a diagnosis of exclusion — if no alternative diagnosis is identified on repeat evaluation, reconsider CHS.","confidence":"consider","summary":"Alternative diagnosis found — treat underlying cause, reconsider CHS if no alternative identified"}'::jsonb
WHERE id = 'chs-organic-found' AND tree_id = 'chs';

-- Node: chs-labs (2 field(s) changed)
UPDATE decision_nodes SET data = '{"type":"info","module":2,"title":"Lab Panel & ECG","body":"**Order baseline labs:**\n• **BMP** — Cr (AKI screening), electrolytes (hypokalemia, hyponatremia)\n• **Magnesium** — often depleted with prolonged vomiting\n• **Lipase** — rule out pancreatitis\n• **Urine drug screen** — confirms cannabis use\n• **Pregnancy test** — all women of reproductive age\n• **Lactate** — if severe dehydration/hypoperfusion\n\n**ECG** — obtain BEFORE administering butyrophenones (haloperidol, droperidol). Check baseline QTc.\n\n**Note:** Ondansetron is typically ineffective in CHS — do not rely on it as first-line therapy.\n\n*Basis:* This is a **consensus panel, not an evidence-derived one** — no study has tested the diagnostic yield or outcome impact of any of these tests in CHS. Individual items trace to the ED-focused reviews by Richards [7] and Gajendran et al. [20] and the 2025 Emergency Medicine Practice review [3]. The pre-treatment ECG requirement is label-driven: the droperidol FDA label directs that **all patients undergo a 12-lead ECG prior to administration** [17], and the haloperidol injection label warns that QTc-prolongation and torsades risk rises with IV administration and higher doses [22]. The ondansetron statement reflects the HaVOC randomised trial, in which haloperidol outperformed ondansetron 8 mg IV [4]; it does **not** mean ondansetron should be withheld — GRACE-4 positions butyrophenones as an addition to usual care including serotonin antagonists [2].","citation":[3,4,7,17,20,22],"next":"chs-ecg","summary":"BMP, Mg, lipase, UDS, pregnancy test — get ECG BEFORE butyrophenones for baseline QTc"}'::jsonb
WHERE id = 'chs-labs' AND tree_id = 'chs';

-- Node: chs-ecg (2 field(s) changed)
UPDATE decision_nodes SET data = '{"type":"question","module":2,"title":"QTc Assessment","body":"Is the baseline QTc prolonged (>500ms)?\n\nButyrophenones (haloperidol, droperidol) carry QTc prolongation risk. If QTc >500ms, avoid butyrophenones and use olanzapine instead.\n\n*Basis:* The 500 ms figure comes from the haloperidol injection FDA label, which states that **a QTc exceeding 500 msec is associated with an increased risk of Torsades de Pointes** [22]. It is a pharmacovigilance-derived threshold, **not a prospectively validated CHS cut-off** — no CHS study has tested outcomes above versus below any QTc value. Scope limitation clinicians must know: the droperidol label uses a **lower** trigger, defining prolongation as QTc >440 msec in males and >450 msec in females and advising against use if the ECG is prolonged [17]; the AAEM position statement reviews the evidence behind droperidol''s boxed warning and supports ED use at low doses with ECG screening [15]. The olanzapine alternative is supported by the ZYPREXA label, which reports no significant difference from placebo in potentially important QT/QTc changes [18].","citation":[15,17,18,22],"options":[{"label":"QTc >500ms","description":"Avoid butyrophenones","urgency":"urgent","next":"chs-qtc-prolonged"},{"label":"QTc normal (<500ms)","description":"Safe to use butyrophenones","next":"chs-iv-access"}],"summary":"QTc >500ms: avoid butyrophenones, use olanzapine instead — no significant QTc prolongation","safetyLevel":"warning"}'::jsonb
WHERE id = 'chs-ecg' AND tree_id = 'chs';

-- Node: chs-iv-access (2 field(s) changed)
UPDATE decision_nodes SET data = '{"type":"info","module":3,"title":"Initial Resuscitation","body":"**Immediate interventions:**\n• IV access — 2 large-bore IVs if severe dehydration\n• **NS bolus 20 mL/kg** for volume repletion\n• NPO (nothing by mouth until vomiting controlled)\n• Cardiac monitor\n\n**GRACE-4 Good Practice Statement:**\n⚠️ **AVOID opioids and benzodiazepines** — opioids worsen gastroparesis, benzodiazepines are ineffective for CHS nausea, and both create dependence risk in this population.\n\nProceed to first-line antiemetic therapy.\n\n*Basis:* The opioid/benzodiazepine avoidance statement is a **GRACE-4 Good Practice Statement** — by GRADE convention these are issued without a formal certainty rating because the supporting evidence was not systematically graded [2]; supporting rationale appears in the EMCrit IBCC cannabis chapter [8] and the 2025 Emergency Medicine Practice review [3]. Independent supporting signal: droperidol use in CHS was associated with markedly reduced opioid administration in a two-centre cohort [16]. The **20 mL/kg NS bolus is extrapolated general resuscitation practice** — no CHS trial has compared fluid volumes, rates or crystalloid type, and no CHS-specific endpoint supports this number. Titrate to perfusion and comorbidity (cardiac, renal) rather than to the stated figure.","citation":[2,3,8,16],"next":"chs-first-line","summary":"NS bolus 20 mL/kg — AVOID opioids and benzodiazepines (worsen gastroparesis, create dependence)","safetyLevel":"warning"}'::jsonb
WHERE id = 'chs-iv-access' AND tree_id = 'chs';

-- Node: chs-first-line (2 field(s) changed)
UPDATE decision_nodes SET data = '{"type":"question","module":3,"title":"First-Line Antiemetic — Butyrophenone Choice","body":"**GRACE-4 Recommendation 1:** Suggest butyrophenones over 5-HT3 antagonists (ondansetron) for CHS (conditional, low certainty evidence).\n\nThe **HaVOC Trial** (2021) demonstrated haloperidol 0.05 mg/kg IV achieved greater nausea VAS reduction at 2 hours compared to ondansetron 4mg IV.\n\nChoose butyrophenone:\n\n*Basis (read before applying):* The primary source is **GRACE-4 recommendation 3a**, whose published wording is: \"in adult ED patients (over the age of 18) presenting to the ED with CHS we suggest the use of haloperidol or droperidol (**in addition to** usual care/serotonin antagonists, e.g., ondansetron) to help with symptom management [**very low** certainty of evidence]\" [2]. Read the guideline text directly before treating ondansetron as contraindicated. The HaVOC trial [4] randomised 33 subjects (30 treated) to haloperidol — with a nested randomisation to **0.05 or 0.1 mg/kg** — versus **ondansetron 8 mg IV**; the primary outcome was the combined reduction in **abdominal pain and nausea** on 10-cm VAS at 2 hours. Haloperidol **at either dose, pooled** was superior (difference 2.3 cm, 95% CI 0.6 to 4.0; P=.01); the trial was **not powered to separate the two haloperidol doses**. Two return visits for acute dystonia occurred, both in the 0.1 mg/kg group. Droperidol has no CHS randomised trial; its use rests on GRACE-4 class-level reasoning [2] and a retrospective two-centre cohort showing reduced length of stay [16].","citation":[2,4,10,16],"options":[{"label":"Haloperidol","description":"HaVOC trial dose: 0.05 mg/kg IV","next":"chs-haloperidol"},{"label":"Droperidol","description":"0.625-2.5 mg IV — faster onset","next":"chs-droperidol"}],"summary":"GRACE-4: butyrophenones over ondansetron — HaVOC trial showed haloperidol superior"}'::jsonb
WHERE id = 'chs-first-line' AND tree_id = 'chs';

-- Node: chs-haloperidol (2 field(s) changed)
UPDATE decision_nodes SET data = '{"type":"info","module":3,"title":"Haloperidol Protocol","body":"[Haloperidol](#/drug/haloperidol/chs) **0.05 mg/kg IV** over 5 minutes (max 5 mg)\n\n**HaVOC Trial Evidence:**\n• Greater nausea VAS reduction at 2 hours vs ondansetron 4mg IV\n• More patients achieved adequate relief\n• Dose is LOWER than standard agitation dosing (0.1 mg/kg)\n\n**May repeat once** at 30 min if inadequate response\n\n**Monitor:** QTc (repeat ECG after dosing), EPS (treat dystonia with diphenhydramine 50mg IV/IM)\n\n*Basis:* Dose derives from the HaVOC randomised trial, which used a nested randomisation to haloperidol **0.05 or 0.1 mg/kg IV** against **ondansetron 8 mg IV**, with a primary outcome of combined abdominal-pain-and-nausea VAS reduction at 2 hours; haloperidol at either dose pooled was superior, and the trial was not powered to separate the two doses [4]. Both acute-dystonia return visits occurred in the 0.1 mg/kg arm [4]. **Regulatory scope limitation clinicians must know:** the haloperidol injection FDA label states plainly that **haloperidol injection is NOT approved for intravenous administration**, and that IV administration and higher-than-recommended doses carry a higher risk of QTc prolongation and Torsades de Pointes; if given IV, the label directs ECG monitoring for QTc prolongation and arrhythmia [22]. IV use for CHS is therefore **off-label**, supported by trial evidence [4] and guideline endorsement [2] rather than by FDA labelling. The 5 mg cap, the 30-minute repeat interval and the diphenhydramine rescue dose are conventional ED practice [3,10] and were **not** protocolised endpoints in HaVOC.","citation":[2,3,4,10,22],"next":"chs-capsaicin","summary":"Haloperidol 0.05 mg/kg IV — LOWER dose than agitation dosing, may repeat once at 30 min","skippable":true}'::jsonb
WHERE id = 'chs-haloperidol' AND tree_id = 'chs';

-- Node: chs-droperidol (2 field(s) changed)
UPDATE decision_nodes SET data = '{"type":"info","module":3,"title":"Droperidol Protocol","body":"[Droperidol](#/drug/droperidol/chs) **0.625-2.5 mg IV**\n\n• **Faster onset** than haloperidol (3-10 min vs 10-20 min)\n• GRACE-4 recommends butyrophenones as a class for CHS\n• More sedating than haloperidol (greater H1/5-HT2A activity)\n\n**May repeat once** at 15-20 min if inadequate response\n\n**Monitor:** QTc (repeat ECG after dosing), blood pressure (alpha-1 blockade can cause hypotension)\n\n*Basis:* **There is no randomised trial of droperidol in CHS.** The recommendation is class-level: GRACE-4 3a names haloperidol *or* droperidol, in addition to usual care, at **very low certainty of evidence** [2]. The strongest direct data are a retrospective two-centre cohort of 211 CHS encounters in which droperidol (median dose **1.25 mg**) was associated with shorter length of stay (409 vs 641 min; adjusted mean ratio 0.76, 95% CI 0.62-0.94), less total medication use and less opioid use, with two mild adverse drug reactions and no dysrhythmias [16] — retrospective, unblinded, and hypothesis-generating, with the authors themselves calling for a randomised dosing trial. The **3-10 minute onset figure is taken from the droperidol FDA label** (\"the onset of action of single intramuscular and intravenous doses is from three to ten minutes\"); the label also sets a maximum recommended **initial** dose of 2.5 mg IM or slow IV with additional 1.25 mg doses given cautiously [17]. **Droperidol carries an FDA boxed warning** for QT prolongation and torsades at or below recommended doses, some cases fatal, and the label directs a 12-lead ECG in all patients before administration [17]; the AAEM position statement reviews the data behind that warning and supports low-dose ED use [15]. No head-to-head trial has compared droperidol with haloperidol for CHS, so the onset and sedation comparisons above are pharmacologic, not outcome-based.","citation":[2,3,10,15,16,17],"next":"chs-capsaicin","summary":"Droperidol 0.625-2.5 mg IV — faster onset than haloperidol, more sedating"}'::jsonb
WHERE id = 'chs-droperidol' AND tree_id = 'chs';

