-- Node: hd-hyperkalemia-severe (1 field(s) changed)
UPDATE decision_nodes SET data = '{"type":"info","module":2,"title":"Severe Hyperkalemia Protocol","body":"**🚨 SEVERE HYPERKALEMIA — ECG changes present**\n\n**1. CARDIAC MEMBRANE STABILIZATION (Immediate):**\n• **Calcium gluconate 1-2 g IV** over 2-3 min\n• Onset: 1-3 minutes\n• Duration: 30-60 minutes\n• May repeat if ECG changes persist\n• Alternative: Calcium chloride 500-1000 mg (central line preferred)\n\n**2. SHIFT K+ INTRACELLULARLY:**\n• **Regular insulin IV — dose by hypoglycemia risk**\n  — Give WITH **25 g dextrose (D50 50 mL)** unless glucose >250 mg/dL\n  — **5 units** (or 0.1 units/kg, max 10) — eGFR <60, dialysis-dependent, pre-treatment glucose <150 mg/dL, weight <60 kg, or no diabetes\n  — **10 units** — normal renal function AND adequate glucose\n  — **Dialysis patients meet the 5-unit criterion → give 5 units**\n  — Onset: 15-30 min, duration 4-6 hr\n  — Check glucose at 30, 60, 120 and 240 min (insulin is renally cleared — hypoglycemia is often delayed)\n• **Albuterol 10-20 mg nebulized** (additive effect)\n  — Onset: 30 min, duration 2 hr\n\n**3. EMERGENT DIALYSIS:**\n• **Call nephrology STAT**\n• Only definitive treatment in ESRD\n• Removes 25-50 mEq K+/hour\n\n**⚠️ NOT emergent therapy:**\n• Kayexalate — hours to work, GI complications\n• Patiromer/SZC — not for acute use","citation":[3,4,18],"next":"hd-electrolyte-disposition","calculatorLinks":[{"id":"hd-hyperkalemia","label":"Hyperkalemia Protocol"}],"summary":"Calcium gluconate 1g IV first, then insulin/dextrose, albuterol neb — emergent dialysis for definitive tx","safetyLevel":"critical"}'::jsonb
WHERE id = 'hd-hyperkalemia-severe' AND tree_id = 'hd-emergencies';

-- Node: hd-hyperkalemia-moderate (2 field(s) changed)
UPDATE decision_nodes SET data = '{"type":"info","module":2,"title":"Moderate Hyperkalemia","body":"**K+ 5.5-6.5, no ECG changes:**\n\n**Management:**\n\n**1. Continuous cardiac monitoring**\n\n**2. Shift therapy:**\n• Regular insulin IV + **25 g dextrose (D50 50 mL)** — **5 units** (or 0.1 units/kg, max 10) if dialysis-dependent, eGFR <60, pre-treatment glucose <150 mg/dL, weight <60 kg, or no diabetes; **10 units** only with normal renal function AND adequate glucose. Dialysis patients → **5 units**.\n• Albuterol 10-20 mg nebulized (additive)\n\n**3. Contact nephrology:**\n• Arrange urgent dialysis\n• May need same-day HD\n\n**Consider calcium gluconate if:**\n• K+ trending up\n• Recent missed dialysis\n• High-risk patient\n\n**Repeat K+ in 1-2 hours**\n\n**Fingerstick glucose at 30 min, 1 h, 2 h, 4 h and 6 h** — insulin is renally cleared, so in dialysis patients hypoglycemia is common and frequently delayed past the ~4 h K+ effect. If glucose <70 mg/dL, give additional dextrose.\n\n**Do NOT delay dialysis** — shift therapy is temporary","citation":[3,4,18],"next":"hd-electrolyte-disposition","summary":"Insulin 5 u (10 u if normal renal function) + 25 g dextrose, kayexalate controversial, arrange urgent dialysis within hours"}'::jsonb
WHERE id = 'hd-hyperkalemia-moderate' AND tree_id = 'hd-emergencies';

-- Node: hop-metabolic-treatment (1 field(s) changed)
UPDATE decision_nodes SET data = '{"type":"info","module":6,"title":"Pre-Intubation Hyperkalemia Treatment","body":"**Stabilize the membrane and shift K+ BEFORE intubation:**\n\n| Intervention | Dose | Onset | Duration | Mechanism |\n|--------------|------|-------|----------|----------|\n| [Calcium gluconate](#/drug/calcium-gluconate/hyperkalemia) | 1-2 g IV over 5-10 min | 1-3 min | 30-60 min | Membrane stabilization |\n| [Calcium chloride](#/drug/calcium-chloride/hyperkalemia) | 1 g IV (central line) | 1-3 min | 30-60 min | 3x more elemental Ca |\n| [Regular insulin](#/drug/insulin-regular/severe hyperkalemia) | 5 units IV (10 units if normal renal function AND adequate glucose) | 15-30 min | 4-6 hours | Intracellular K+ shift |\n| **Dextrose 50%** | 50 mL (25g) IV | -- | -- | Prevent hypoglycemia |\n| [Albuterol](#/drug/albuterol-neb/hyperkalemia) | 10-20 mg nebulized | 15-30 min | 2-4 hours | Intracellular K+ shift |\n\n**Calcium FIRST** - Stabilizes myocardium within minutes while other therapies take effect.\n\n**Insulin dose by hypoglycemia risk** - Give 5 units (or 0.1 units/kg, max 10) if eGFR <60, dialysis-dependent, pre-treatment glucose <150 mg/dL, weight <60 kg, or no diabetes. Give 10 units only with normal renal function AND adequate glucose. Always pair with 25 g dextrose (D50 50 mL) unless glucose >250 mg/dL.\n\n**Check glucose at 30, 60, 120 and 240 min** - hypoglycemia is the main iatrogenic harm.","citation":[8],"next":"hop-metabolic-paralytic","summary":"Calcium, insulin/dextrose, bicarb BEFORE induction if severe hyperkalemia — correct hypocalcemia too","safetyLevel":"critical"}'::jsonb
WHERE id = 'hop-metabolic-treatment' AND tree_id = 'hop-killers';

-- Node: lems-start (2 field(s) changed)
UPDATE decision_nodes SET data = '{"type":"info","module":1,"title":"Lambert-Eaton Myasthenic Syndrome (LEMS)","body":"**LEMS** is a **presynaptic** autoimmune disorder of the neuromuscular junction. Antibodies against **P/Q-type voltage-gated calcium channels (VGCC)** on the motor nerve terminal reduce calcium influx and therefore reduce acetylcholine release. The postsynaptic receptor is normal — the defect is upstream.\n\n**The mechanistic signature:** one nerve impulse releases too little ACh (weakness, low resting CMAP), but **rapid repetitive activity lets calcium build up**, transiently boosting ACh release. That is why strength and reflexes **improve with use** — *facilitation*, the mirror image of myasthenia gravis fatigability.\n\n**~50-60% is paraneoplastic**, almost always tied to **small cell lung cancer (SCLC)** — and LEMS often **precedes the cancer diagnosis by months to years.** The rest is autoimmune/idiopathic.\n\n**Classic patient:** an older smoker with proximal leg weakness, a dry mouth, and hard-to-elicit reflexes.\n\n*Basis: presynaptic anti-P/Q VGCC pathophysiology and the SCLC association are established in peer-reviewed reviews of LEMS [2,3]. The tumour-association figure is a range across cohorts — Titulaer 2011 and Gilhus 2011 both state \"half\" (~50%), while the classic 50-case Newsom-Davis series found a 62% risk of underlying SCLC that declines sharply after 2 years [17]. Treat \"~50-60%\" as a cohort-dependent estimate, not a fixed rate. Epidemiology and mechanism, not a treatment directive — no ED action follows from this node alone.*","citation":[2,3,17],"next":"lems-triad","images":[{"src":"data:image/svg+xml;utf8,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20440%20220%22%20font-family%3D%22sans-serif%22%3E%3Crect%20width%3D%22440%22%20height%3D%22220%22%20fill%3D%22%230f172a%22%2F%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%22220%22%20y%3D%2224%22%20fill%3D%22%23e2e8f0%22%20font-size%3D%2215%22%20font-weight%3D%22bold%22%20text-anchor%3D%22middle%22%3ELEMS%20vs%20MG%20%E2%80%94%20opposite%20NMJ%20defects%3C%2Ftext%3E%3Crect%20x%3D%2230%22%20y%3D%2244%22%20width%3D%22170%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20rx%3D%2210%22%20fill%3D%22%231e293b%22%20stroke%3D%22%23fbbf24%22%2F%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%22115%22%20y%3D%2266%22%20fill%3D%22%23fbbf24%22%20font-size%3D%2212%22%20font-weight%3D%22bold%22%20text-anchor%3D%22middle%22%3ELEMS%20(presynaptic)%3C%2Ftext%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%22115%22%20y%3D%2290%22%20fill%3D%22%23cbd5e1%22%20font-size%3D%2210%22%20text-anchor%3D%22middle%22%3Eanti-VGCC%2C%20low%20ACh%20release%3C%2Ftext%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%22115%22%20y%3D%22112%22%20fill%3D%22%2334d399%22%20font-size%3D%2211%22%20text-anchor%3D%22middle%22%3EIMPROVES%20with%20use%3C%2Ftext%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%22115%22%20y%3D%22132%22%20fill%3D%22%23cbd5e1%22%20font-size%3D%2210%22%20text-anchor%3D%22middle%22%3Eproximal%20legs%20%26gt%3B%20ocular%3C%2Ftext%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%22115%22%20y%3D%22152%22%20fill%3D%22%23cbd5e1%22%20font-size%3D%2210%22%20text-anchor%3D%22middle%22%3Edry%20mouth%20%2F%20autonomic%3C%2Ftext%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%22115%22%20y%3D%22172%22%20fill%3D%22%23cbd5e1%22%20font-size%3D%2210%22%20text-anchor%3D%22middle%22%3ESCLC%20link%3C%2Ftext%3E%3Crect%20x%3D%22240%22%20y%3D%2244%22%20width%3D%22170%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20rx%3D%2210%22%20fill%3D%22%231e293b%22%20stroke%3D%22%2360a5fa%22%2F%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%22325%22%20y%3D%2266%22%20fill%3D%22%2360a5fa%22%20font-size%3D%2212%22%20font-weight%3D%22bold%22%20text-anchor%3D%22middle%22%3EMG%20(postsynaptic)%3C%2Ftext%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%22325%22%20y%3D%2290%22%20fill%3D%22%23cbd5e1%22%20font-size%3D%2210%22%20text-anchor%3D%22middle%22%3Eanti-AChR%2FMuSK%3C%2Ftext%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%22325%22%20y%3D%22112%22%20fill%3D%22%23f87171%22%20font-size%3D%2211%22%20text-anchor%3D%22middle%22%3EWORSENS%20with%20use%3C%2Ftext%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%22325%22%20y%3D%22132%22%20fill%3D%22%23cbd5e1%22%20font-size%3D%2210%22%20text-anchor%3D%22middle%22%3Eocular%20%2F%20bulbar%20onset%3C%2Ftext%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%22325%22%20y%3D%22152%22%20fill%3D%22%23cbd5e1%22%20font-size%3D%2210%22%20text-anchor%3D%22middle%22%3Ereflexes%20normal%3C%2Ftext%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%22325%22%20y%3D%22172%22%20fill%3D%22%23cbd5e1%22%20font-size%3D%2210%22%20text-anchor%3D%22middle%22%3Ethymoma%20link%3C%2Ftext%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E","alt":"Two panels contrasting LEMS (presynaptic, anti-VGCC, improves with use, proximal legs, autonomic, SCLC) with myasthenia gravis (postsynaptic, anti-AChR, worsens with use, ocular onset, thymoma).","caption":"LEMS is the mirror image of MG: presynaptic, improves with use, proximal, autonomic, SCLC-linked. (Original schematic.)"}],"summary":"Presynaptic anti-VGCC NMJ disorder; improves with use (facilitation). ~50-60% paraneoplastic with SCLC, often preceding the cancer."}'::jsonb
WHERE id = 'lems-start' AND tree_id = 'lambert-eaton';

-- Node: lems-triad (2 field(s) changed)
UPDATE decision_nodes SET data = '{"type":"question","module":2,"title":"The LEMS Triad","body":"**(1) Proximal muscle weakness** — the dominant complaint. **Legs > arms**, symmetric. Difficulty rising from a chair, climbing stairs, waddling gait. Atrophy is mild relative to weakness.\n\n**(2) Autonomic dysfunction** — present in 80-96%. **Dry mouth is the most common and often earliest** symptom. Also constipation, erectile dysfunction, orthostatic hypotension, blurred vision, reduced sweating.\n\n**(3) Hypo/areflexia that AUGMENTS after exercise** — a reflex that is absent at rest may reappear after ~10-15 seconds of sustained contraction (**post-exercise facilitation**). This is the bedside hallmark and the **opposite of MG fatigability.**\n\n**Ocular/bulbar:** present but milder and later than MG. Bulbar/ocular *onset* should make you reconsider MG.\n\n*Basis: the triad and its component frequencies come from clinical case series, not trials. The 50-patient Newsom-Davis series found proximal lower-limb weakness in 100%, depressed reflexes in 92%, post-tetanic potentiation in 78% and dry mouth in 74% [17]; a separate Mayo series characterises the autonomic phenotype and its serologic correlates [16]. Individual percentages vary by cohort and by how \"autonomic dysfunction\" is ascertained (symptom questionnaire vs formal autonomic testing) — the quoted 80-96% is at the upper end of that literature. This node is a recognition aid; it does not by itself establish the diagnosis, which requires serology plus electrodiagnostics [2].*","citation":[2,16,17],"options":[{"label":"Proximal leg weakness + dry mouth + reflexes improve with use","description":"The classic LEMS triad — confirm and hunt for cancer","next":"lems-diagnosis"},{"label":"Ocular/bulbar onset, fatigable, reflexes normal","description":"Looks more like myasthenia gravis — switch tracks","next":"lems-mg-compare"}],"summary":"Triad: proximal (legs>arms) weakness + autonomic (dry mouth) + reflexes that augment with exercise. Facilitation = bedside hallmark."}'::jsonb
WHERE id = 'lems-triad' AND tree_id = 'lambert-eaton';

-- Node: lems-mg-compare (2 field(s) changed)
UPDATE decision_nodes SET data = '{"type":"info","module":2,"title":"LEMS vs Myasthenia Gravis","body":"| Feature | **LEMS** | **[Myasthenia gravis](#/tree/myasthenia-gravis)** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Weakness pattern | **Proximal** (legs > arms) | **Ocular / bulbar** onset |\n| Response to use | **Improves** (facilitation) | **Worsens** (fatigability) |\n| Reflexes | Hypo/areflexic; **augment post-exercise** | Normal |\n| Autonomic | **Prominent** (dry mouth, ED, orthostasis) | Absent |\n| Antibody | **Anti-P/Q VGCC** (~85-95%) | Anti-AChR / anti-MuSK |\n| Tumor | **SCLC** (~50-60%) | **Thymoma** (~10-15%) |\n| RNS | High-freq / post-exercise **increment** | Low-freq **decrement** |\n\n**If the picture is ocular/bulbar-onset and fatigable, work it up as MG.** If it is proximal, autonomic, and improves with use, it is LEMS until proven otherwise.\n\n*Basis: the weakness-distribution contrast in row 1 is drawn from a direct head-to-head comparison of MG and LEMS patients [18]; the RNS row (decrement vs increment) is drawn from direct RNS comparison studies [13]; the antibody, autonomic and tumour rows from LEMS reviews [2]. Note the table states discriminating tendencies, not absolute rules — overlap exists (LEMS can have ocular/bulbar features, MG can show low-frequency decrement in only about half of patients [13]). Use it to direct testing, not to substitute for serology and electrodiagnostics.*","citation":[2,13,18],"next":"lems-diagnosis","summary":"LEMS: proximal, improves with use, autonomic, anti-VGCC, SCLC, RNS increment. MG: ocular/bulbar, fatigable, anti-AChR, thymoma, RNS decrement.","skippable":true}'::jsonb
WHERE id = 'lems-mg-compare' AND tree_id = 'lambert-eaton';

-- Node: lems-diagnosis (2 field(s) changed)
UPDATE decision_nodes SET data = '{"type":"info","module":3,"title":"Diagnosis","body":"**Three pillars: clinical triad + serology + electrodiagnostics.**\n\n**Serology:**\n- **Anti-P/Q-type VGCC antibodies** — positive in ~85-95% (titers >~30 pmol/L support the diagnosis in context). ~10-15% seronegative.\n- **N-type VGCC antibodies are NOT useful in isolation** (low sensitivity, nonspecific) — do not diagnose LEMS off an isolated N-type result.\n- **SOX1 antibodies** (~64% in SCLC-LEMS, ~95% specific) — a useful paraneoplastic flag.\n\n**Electrodiagnostics (repetitive nerve stimulation):**\n- Low **resting** CMAP amplitude\n- **Decrement** at low-frequency (2-5 Hz) stimulation\n- **>60% (often >100%) INCREMENT at high-frequency (20-50 Hz) or after 10 s of maximal contraction** — post-exercise facilitation, the electrodiagnostic signature.\n\nSerology and EMG must be interpreted together with the clinical picture.\n\n*Basis: the >60% increment threshold is not consensus opinion — it comes from a direct comparison of 34 LEMS patients against 40 normal controls and 538 RNS studies in myasthenia gravis, where a 60% increment on post-exercise facilitation or high-rate stimulation gave 97% sensitivity for LEMS and 99% specificity against MG, versus 85% sensitivity for the older 100% \"gold standard\" [13]; the same group re-confirmed the 60% figure in a 50-year reappraisal [13]. The advice against relying on isolated N-type VGCC antibodies comes from a Mayo Clinic laboratory series [4]. The SOX1 figures are separately sourced: ~64% positivity in SCLC-LEMS versus 0% in idiopathic LEMS [14], and ~95% specificity for discriminating SCLC-LEMS from non-tumour LEMS [15]. The anti-P/Q VGCC sensitivity is a cohort-dependent range — reviews quote ~85% [3] up to the ~95% upper bound shown here [2]; a negative antibody does not exclude LEMS (~10-15% seronegative). Diagnostic thresholds, not treatment directives — confirm assay units and cutoffs with your own laboratory.*","citation":[2,4,13,14,15],"next":"lems-cancer","summary":"Anti-P/Q VGCC (~85-95%); RNS shows high-frequency/post-exercise increment >60%. N-type antibody not useful alone; SOX1 flags SCLC."}'::jsonb
WHERE id = 'lems-diagnosis' AND tree_id = 'lambert-eaton';

-- Node: lems-cancer (2 field(s) changed)
UPDATE decision_nodes SET data = '{"type":"info","module":4,"title":"Hunt the Small Cell Lung Cancer","body":"**Every new LEMS diagnosis triggers a malignancy hunt** — treating the SCLC often improves the neurologic syndrome, and LEMS may be the first sign of an otherwise-occult tumor.\n\n**Imaging:**\n- **CT chest** first-line; if negative, **FDG-PET/CT.**\n- If initial imaging is negative, **repeat screening every 3-6 months for at least 2 years** (high-risk: every 3 months). *FLAGGED: confirm the surveillance interval against your institution / current guidance.*\n\n**Risk-stratify with DELTA-P** (1 point each, assessed at/within 3 months of onset): age ≥50, current smoking, weight loss ≥5%, bulbar involvement, erectile dysfunction, Karnofsky <70.\n- Score 0-1 → SCLC risk near 0-2.6%\n- Scores 4/5/6 → ~93.5% / 96.6% / 100%\n\nHigh DELTA-P → expedite PET and oncology.\n\n*Basis: the DELTA-P items and the probabilities shown here are the DERIVATION-cohort figures from a 107-patient Dutch cohort validated in 112 British patients (AUC 94.4% / 94.6%) [6]. An independent PROSPECTIVE cohort of 87 newly diagnosed patients found meaningfully weaker discrimination — AUC 82.5%, and a score of 1 carried an 18.8% SCLC rate rather than the 0-2.6% quoted for scores 0-1, with only weight loss, smoking and age >=50 remaining independent predictors [8]. Read the low-risk band as \"lower risk\", not \"rule out\", and do not use DELTA-P to skip imaging. The imaging strategy (CT chest, then FDG-PET/CT) follows a European task-force recommendation on tumour screening in paraneoplastic syndromes [5]; the 2-year surveillance window is supported by the observation that SCLC became evident within 2 years of LEMS onset in 20 of 21 tumour cases [17], but the exact 3-6 month interval is expert consensus, not trial-derived — hence the FLAG above.*","citation":[5,6,8,17],"next":"lems-avoid","calculatorLinks":[{"id":"lems-delta-p","label":"DELTA-P SCLC Risk Score"}],"summary":"CT chest -> PET; repeat surveillance for >=2 years if negative. DELTA-P (age>=50, smoking, weight loss, bulbar, ED, low Karnofsky) stratifies SCLC risk."}'::jsonb
WHERE id = 'lems-cancer' AND tree_id = 'lambert-eaton';

-- Node: lems-avoid (2 field(s) changed)
UPDATE decision_nodes SET data = '{"type":"info","module":5,"title":"Drugs to AVOID — Highest ED-Safety Yield","body":"**This is where the ED can harm a LEMS patient.** Several common drugs worsen presynaptic NMJ transmission and can precipitate respiratory failure.\n\n**Avoid / use with extreme caution:**\n- **Neuromuscular blockers (depolarizing AND non-depolarizing)** — profound, **prolonged paralysis**. LEMS is frequently *first discovered* via unexpected prolonged blockade after anesthesia. If RSI is unavoidable, anticipate marked dose sensitivity and delayed recovery; favor a sugammadex-reversible agent. *FLAGGED: paralytic strategy is a clinical-judgment call.*\n- **Aminoglycosides, fluoroquinolones** — impair NMJ transmission.\n- **IV magnesium** — competes with presynaptic calcium; can precipitate severe weakness.\n- **Calcium-channel blockers (esp. verapamil, diltiazem)** — documented precipitants of respiratory failure in LEMS.\n- **Iodinated IV contrast** — reported to exacerbate weakness.\n\n**Practical ED move:** flag the chart, avoid these agents, and if intubation is truly required, plan for prolonged paralysis and reversibility.\n\n*Basis: this list is built on observational and case-based evidence, not randomised trials — no trial has been or will be run deliberately exposing LEMS patients to these agents. Neuromuscular-blocker sensitivity is the best supported item: a 37-patient / 60-surgery perioperative series concluded that LEMS patients have increased sensitivity to neuromuscular blocking drugs, that 4 patients (11%) had respiratory complications, and that 3 of those were undiagnosed or untreated at the time and developed weakness after a blocker was given [11]. The calcium-channel-blocker warning rests on a case of intubation-requiring respiratory failure after combined diltiazem plus verapamil, plus a literature review that at the time identified only 5 published cases of definite cardiac CCB harm across LEMS and MG [12] — a real but rare signal. Aminoglycosides, fluoroquinolones and magnesium are grouped from the general drug-induced neuromuscular-blockade literature, which is largely derived from myasthenia gravis and extrapolated to LEMS . The iodinated-contrast item is the weakest link in the list, supported by isolated reports only. Treat the whole node as risk-avoidance guidance whose strength varies item by item; the paralytic decision itself remains a clinical judgement, as flagged above.*","citation":[11,12],"next":"lems-resp","summary":"AVOID: NM blockers (prolonged paralysis), aminoglycosides/fluoroquinolones, IV magnesium, CCBs (verapamil/diltiazem), IV contrast. These precipitate respiratory failure.","safetyLevel":"critical"}'::jsonb
WHERE id = 'lems-avoid' AND tree_id = 'lambert-eaton';

-- Node: lems-resp (2 field(s) changed)
UPDATE decision_nodes SET data = '{"type":"info","module":6,"title":"Respiratory Monitoring","body":"**Frank respiratory failure is less common than in MG but does occur — and is often iatrogenic** (see drugs to avoid). It is the only truly emergent LEMS complication.\n\n**Monitor:**\n- **FVC and NIF**; bedside **single-breath count** (cannot reach 20 = concerning)\n- Bulbar safety: cough strength, secretion handling, swallow\n\n**Pulse oximetry is a poor/late indicator** of neuromuscular respiratory strength — do not be reassured by a normal SpO2.\n\n*FLAGGED: specific FVC/NIF intubation thresholds here are extrapolated from the vetted [Myasthenia Gravis](#/tree/myasthenia-gravis) consult, as LEMS-specific cutoffs are not well established. Use the MG thresholds and trend the patient.*\n\n*Basis: there is no LEMS-specific validated respiratory threshold in the literature and no prospective study of FVC/NIF cutoffs in LEMS — the numbers this node points you to are borrowed from myasthenia gravis, which is why the FLAG above exists and why the linked calculator is explicitly labelled \"MG thresholds\". The claim that respiratory failure occurs but is uncommon and often iatrogenic is supported by a published case report with literature review of acute respiratory failure as a LEMS presentation [10] and by the perioperative series in which respiratory complications clustered in patients given neuromuscular blockers [11]; the underlying general-review framing is tertiary [1]. Use these parameters as a trend, not as a validated LEMS decision rule, and escalate on the clinical trajectory.*","citation":[1,10,11],"next":"lems-treatment","calculatorLinks":[{"id":"mg-fvc-nif","label":"FVC/NIF Respiratory Monitor (MG thresholds)"}],"summary":"Crisis less common than MG but occurs, often iatrogenic. Trend FVC/NIF + single-breath count; SpO2 is late. Thresholds mirror the MG consult.","safetyLevel":"warning"}'::jsonb
WHERE id = 'lems-resp' AND tree_id = 'lambert-eaton';

-- Node: lems-treatment (2 field(s) changed)
UPDATE decision_nodes SET data = '{"type":"info","module":7,"title":"Treatment","body":"**Symptomatic:**\n- **First-line: amifampridine (3,4-diaminopyridine, 3,4-DAP)** — blocks presynaptic K+ channels, prolonging the action potential and boosting calcium influx / ACh release. **Start 15-30 mg/day PO in 3-4 divided doses; titrate by 5 mg every 3-4 days; single dose ≤20 mg. FDA max (Firdapse, US labeling updated May 2024): 100 mg/day for patients >45 kg (was 80 mg/day); for patients <45 kg start 7.5-15 mg/day, max 50 mg/day.** FDA-approved for LEMS. *FLAGGED: confirm formulation (phosphate vs base) and current institutional dosing.*\n- **Pyridostigmine** — adjunct; **less effective than in MG**, sometimes combined with amifampridine.\n- **Guanidine** — only if amifampridine unavailable (renal/marrow toxicity).\n\n**Immunotherapy (refractory/severe, usually inpatient with neurology):** IVIG (~2 g/kg over 2-5 days), corticosteroids, plasma exchange; steroid-sparing maintenance (azathioprine, mycophenolate, rituximab).\n\n**Treat the underlying cancer** — for paraneoplastic LEMS, anti-tumor therapy is central and often the most effective intervention.\n\n*Therapy is rarely initiated in the ED; the ED role is recognition, safety (drug avoidance), respiratory monitoring, and coordinating neuro/onc.*\n\n*Basis: amifampridine is the only FDA-approved therapy for LEMS and the authoritative dosing source is the manufacturer''s current US prescribing information for FIRDAPSE (amifampridine phosphate), which is publicly reviewable on DailyMed [21]. **Read the numbers above against that label before prescribing** — the label is revised periodically, it specifies different starting doses, titration increments, maximum single doses and maximum daily doses for adults/patients at or above 45 kg versus paediatric patients below 45 kg, and it separately reduces the starting dose in renal impairment, hepatic impairment and NAT2 poor metabolisers. It also differs from the older 3,4-DAP base-form literature, which is why the formulation FLAG above matters. The efficacy claim for amifampridine and the relative weakness of pyridostigmine rest on a drug review [7] and on a Cochrane systematic review of LEMS treatment [9], which found only small randomised trials with short follow-up and surrogate (muscle-strength/CMAP) endpoints; the immunotherapy regimens are extrapolated from small trials and clinical practice rather than large comparative studies [3]. No dose in this node should be initiated from the ED without neurology involvement and a direct label check.*","citation":[3,7,9,21],"next":"lems-differential","summary":"Amifampridine (3,4-DAP) 15-30 mg TID first-line; pyridostigmine adjunct. IVIG/steroids/PLEX for severe. Treat the SCLC. ED rarely initiates therapy."}'::jsonb
WHERE id = 'lems-treatment' AND tree_id = 'lambert-eaton';

-- Node: lems-differential (2 field(s) changed)
UPDATE decision_nodes SET data = '{"type":"info","module":8,"title":"Differential","body":"- **[Myasthenia gravis](#/tree/myasthenia-gravis)** — closest mimic; fatigability vs facilitation, ocular/bulbar vs proximal, autonomic features, antibody, RNS decrement vs increment.\n- **[Guillain-Barré](#/tree/guillain-barre)** — ascending weakness + areflexia, but with sensory involvement, CSF albuminocytologic dissociation, and a demyelinating EMG.\n- **[Botulism](#/tree/botulism)** — also **presynaptic** with **autonomic** features (dry mouth, ileus), but **descending**, cranial-nerve-first, with **dilated/poorly reactive pupils** and an exposure history (food/wound/infant). Botulism RNS can also increment — the clinical tempo and pupil/bulbar predominance separate it.\n- **Inflammatory myopathy / polymyositis** — proximal weakness but normal reflexes, elevated CK, no facilitation, no autonomic signs.\n- Also: ALS, inclusion-body myositis, lumbar canal stenosis — LEMS is initially misdiagnosed as one of these in up to ~60% of cases.\n\n*Basis: the LEMS-versus-MG discriminators are supported by a direct comparison of weakness distribution between the two conditions [18] and by LEMS reviews [1,2]; the caution against over-reading an isolated antibody result is sourced separately [4]. The remaining entries (GBS, botulism, inflammatory myopathy, ALS, IBM, canal stenosis) are pattern-recognition contrasts drawn from narrative review, not from a prospective diagnostic-accuracy study of this differential — no validated decision rule discriminates them. The \"~60% initially misdiagnosed\" figure is a frequently repeated review-level estimate rather than a pooled measurement, and the underlying case series vary widely in referral pattern and era; treat it as an argument for keeping LEMS on the list, not as a performance statistic.*","citation":[1,2,4,18],"next":"lems-disposition","summary":"MG (fatigable/decrement), GBS (sensory/CSF/demyelinating), botulism (descending/pupils/exposure), myopathy (normal reflexes, high CK). Often misdiagnosed early."}'::jsonb
WHERE id = 'lems-differential' AND tree_id = 'lambert-eaton';

